moving on in this morning's money watch  the newest inflation numbers have just  come out they're starting to show the  impact of falling gas prices the  consumer price index shows prices rose  eight and a half percent in july  compared to a year ago this follows  june's inflation rate of 9.1 percent  which was the highest in more than 40  years the national average of a price of  for a gallon of gas has fallen nearly 70  cents just over the past month cbs news  business analyst jill schlesinger is  here to help us break this all down jill  good morning 8.5  that's a significant reduction yeah it  may be that inflation peaked in the  month of june because these july numbers  are somewhat better than expected not  fantastic news yet but we were thinking  8.7 percent on an annualized basis eight  and a half is good the other part of  this report that you as you mentioned  gas and energy prices that pushes the  numbers down pretty dramatically and if  we look at the gas index for example  down 7.7  over the last month obviously right  since gas peaked in june we're down a  dollar a gallon since then but when you  look at the year over year and you want  to know why people are still complaining  that gas index is still up 44  when we pull out gas and all energy as  well as food the rest of the inflation  picture is still simmering so we really  have to pay attention to that  specifically shelter which continues to  be a big driver of inflation so how will  we know that prices have peaked i mean  what's the guarantee that they won't  just go back up next month well i think  that's a great question because the war  in ukraine is the real unknown that was  what pushed oil prices above 120 a  barrel now we're back at 90. food prices  also increase because so much of the  bread basket of europe is derived from  ukraine i think that if the war does not  create another real shortage that we  could believe that over the next few  months we have seen the peak the end of  the year we should come down towards  seven maybe six percent but just  remember gang before we started this  inflationary period the inflation rate  in the united states was just under two  percent so these are big changes what  does it mean for a potential recession  well i mean look when we have high  periods of inflation and the federal  reserve is actively fighting that  inflation by raising interest rates  those are the conditions that usually  will lead to this broad slowdown called  a recession it does not appear we're in  a recession right this moment i mean  frankly we had a half a million jobs  created in the last month and an economy  that can create a half a million jobs is  not usually one that is classically in a  recession so i think that maybe for  another month we're going to sort of put  that recession talk off to the table  half a million jobs created but on the  flip side unemployment is now at more  than a 50-year low now what does that  mean  when it comes to the inflation rate i'll  tell you what's interesting about the  story of the labor market and that is  wages wages have been going up at a  pretty good clip so  5.2  from a year ago and businesses are  saying we have to pay more and that's  hard it's why it's hard to hire folks  but that wage component to me is very  important because when you think about  5.2  of a wage gain but inflation's at eight  and a half percent there's a gas  so people are falling behind does this  drop in the inflation rate mean that the  fed's interest rate hikes are working  and what does it mean for future hikes  well i mean yes and no i think that it  is that is clear that the fed's rate  hikes are probably having an impact on  the overall economy what's a bigger  impact right now on these numbers has to  be energy and food and that's probably  more war related so i think in the  months ahead the fed is going to keep  raising rates and i think that they are  going to have to because all those other  items without food and energy are still  high and the fed is probably going to go  a half or three quarters now still on  our way out here one last question what  is going on with credit card debt  well you know that gap that we talked  about where someone's making uh let's  say 5.2 more than last year but  everything cost eight and a half percent  more this year well guess what first  people start spending down their savings  people had a lot of savings during covid  there were stimulus checks there was  excess unemployment insurance there was  child tax credit so they built up  savings they've spent those savings down  but now we know once those savings are  down credit card debt has increased  ballooned credit card balances are up 13  from a year ago that is the biggest  annual pace in more than two decades and  just to be clear who's borrowing the  most the people you would imagine young  people they don't have savings built up  right and people who already have low  credit scores so we've got to keep an  eye on that because as the economy does  slow down those folks are still going to  be struggling with those credit card  balances wow so it's not just you at  home if you've got that credit card bill  going up all right jill slesner thank  you very much we appreciate it
 

